  "note": "LabelSets that name no Apple row. THIS LIST MAY ONLY SHRINK.\n\n`check-labelsets-are-derived.py` proves, offline and per locale, that a LabelSet's members are the values Apple pins at its row -- but only for the sets that DECLARE one. `check-new-labelsets-name-a-row.py` requires a declaration of every set added from now on, measured against the merge base. Between them sits this census: the sets that predate both rules. Nothing asked whether these work in every language, and the answer is UNMEASURED rather than no.\n\nThe list was 58 when it was written and is 61. The three added on 2026-09-20 -- `eventListColumnM`, `eventListColumnName`, `eventListColumnPosition` -- are not new LabelSets: they name no row and always did, and the guard's own parser could not see them because their declarations close on the same line as their last argument. The guard reads them through `check-labelsets-are-derived.py`'s parser now, and it refuses an addition only when the set named a row at the merge base.\n\nEach entry carries what `Scripts/derive_label_variants.py` says about it, so the work is sized rather than guessed. `value` and `namespace` mean a row was found; `ambiguous` means several resolve and somebody has to pick the one that MEANS this control; `no-row` means an absence proof is what closes it.\n\nDO NOT CLOSE THESE MECHANICALLY. Measured while writing this file: `trackTypeMaster`'s best candidate is `ChineseOnly_TI_BassAmpLabel_Master_lowercase`, a Bass Amp label whose value happens to match and whose meaning does not. A row that resolves is not the row a control is named by.",
